      "parsed_output": {
        "evidence": "Verified against the actual worktree source (src/main/java/org/zeroturnaround/zip/ZipUtil.java):\n- Primary sink exercised by this PoV: ZipUtil$Unpacker.process (private inner class, lines ~1149-1170). It computes `File file = new File(outputDir, name);` directly from the (identity-)mapped zip entry name, then does `FileUtils.forceMkdir(file.getParentFile()); FileUtils.copy(in, file);` with zero canonical-path/containment check against outputDir. Reached from the public APIs ZipUtil.unpack(File,File) [line 994/996], unpack(File,File,Charset), unpack(File,File,NameMapper), unpack(File,File,NameMapper,Charset) [line 938/939], and the InputStream overloads unpack(InputStream,File[,...]) [lines 1060/1061, 1076/1077, 1094/1096] — all four alert traces in vulnerability #1 collapse into this single sink (ZipUtil.java:1149-1160, `new File(...)`/`file` at ~1152/1157/1159).\n- Second distinct sink (alert vulnerability #2): ZipUtil$BackslashUnpacker.process (public static class, lines 1181-1229). It splits the (mapped) entry name on '\\\\' and builds `new File(parentDirectory, dirs[i])` per segment (lines ~1214/1220) and, for names without a backslash, `new File(outputDir, name)` (line ~1225) — again no containment check. Reached via ZipUtil.iterate(zip, new ZipUtil.BackslashUnpacker(outputDir)) (used e.g. by the existing testUnpackBackslashes test), independent of Unpacker/Unwraper.\n- Third distinct sink (alert vulnerability #3): ZipUtil$Unwraper.process (lines 1249-1267), same pattern: `File file = new File(outputDir, name);` (line ~1260) then FileUtils.forceMkdir/FileUtils.copy (lines ~1265/1267), reached from the public APIs unwrap(File,File[,NameMapper]) [lines 1011/1012, 1029/1031] and unwrap(InputStream,File[,NameMapper]) [lines 1111/1112, 1129/1131].\n- Trust boundary: `name`/`zipEntry.getName()` comes from an untrusted, attacker-supplied zip archive (the \"zip : File\" / entry-name source in the alert); it crosses into filesystem write operations (FileUtils.forceMkdir/FileUtils.copy) with no validation. Confirmed empirically: current code writes the PoV's traversal entry to $TMPDIR/zipslip-pov-escaped-marker/pwned.txt, fully outside the intended outputDir, and this was fixed by adding a canonical-path prefix check (tested by temporarily patching Unpacker.process and re-running the PoV, which then throws ZipException and exits 1); the patch was reverted afterward so product code is unmodified.",
        "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "ZipUtil.unpack(maliciousZip, outputDir) extracts the traversal entry to a path computed outside outputDir (fixed marker file at $TMPDIR/zipslip-pov-escaped-marker/pwned.txt, computed as outputDir + enough \"../\" segments to reach the fs root plus the marker's absolute path). The PoV verifies the marker file exists with the expected content and exits 0. After a correct fix, the entry is rejected (e.g. exception) or normalized/skipped, the marker file is never created, and the PoV exits 1.",
        "notes": "Targeted alert vulnerability #1 (the 4-trace group ending in ZipUtil$Unpacker.process, lines ~1149-1160), via the simplest and most commonly used public entry point ZipUtil.unpack(File, File), because all four traces in that group share the exact same root cause (missing containment check in Unpacker.process) and merely differ in which public overload is called to reach it — exercising one exercises the shared defect. Vulnerabilities #2 (BackslashUnpacker, IdentityNameMapper/name.split trace) and #3 (Unwraper, unwrap() traces) are structurally identical missing-check defects in sibling inner classes of the same file and are called out explicitly in `evidence`/`regression_hint` so the patcher fixes all three sinks, not only the one exercised by this PoV. I validated the PoV's patch-sensitivity by temporarily adding a canonical-path containment check to Unpacker.process, re-running the exact same pov_command (which then exited 1 with a thrown ZipException), and then reverting that temporary change so the worktree's product source is back to its original, unmodified state (verified via `git status --porcelain`, which now shows only the untracked `.security-pipeline/` directory).",
        "pov_command": "mvn -q -o -DskipTests compile && SLF4J=/root/.m2/repository/org/slf4j/slf4j-api/1.6.6/slf4j-api-1.6.6.jar && mkdir -p /tmp/pov-classes && javac -cp \"target/classes:$SLF4J\" -d /tmp/pov-classes .security-pipeline/pov/ZipSlipPOV.java && java -cp \"/tmp/pov-classes:target/classes:$SLF4J\" ZipSlipPOV",
        "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/ZipSlipPOV.java",
        "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce at every sink: for every extracted entry, the canonical/resolved path of the destination file must be equal to, or a descendant of, the canonical path of outputDir (`resolved.getCanonicalPath().startsWith(outputDir.getCanonicalPath() + File.separator)` or equivalent using java.nio.file.Path#normalize + startsWith, not raw string prefix matching which is itself bypassable by sibling-directory names like `outputDir-evil`). If the check fails, the entry must be rejected (throw) or skipped — never partially written.\nSites that all need the same guard (a fix touching only one is incomplete):\n  1. ZipUtil$Unpacker.process — used by unpack(File,File[,Charset|NameMapper|NameMapper+Charset]) and unpack(InputStream,File[,Charset|NameMapper|NameMapper+Charset]).\n  2. ZipUtil$BackslashUnpacker.process — used explicitly via ZipUtil.iterate(..., new ZipUtil.BackslashUnpacker(outputDir)); note this class has two separate file-construction branches (backslash-split loop AND the plain `new File(outputDir, name)` else-branch) that both need the check.\n  3. ZipUtil$Unwraper.process — used by unwrap(File,File[,NameMapper]) and unwrap(InputStream,File[,NameMapper]).\nKnown bypass variants the fix must also block, not just plain \"../\":\n  - Absolute entry names (e.g. \"/etc/passwd\" or \"C:\\\\...\"), where `new File(outputDir, name)` ignores outputDir entirely because File(File,String) treats an absolute child path as absolute.\n  - Mixed/alternate separators — backslash traversal (\"..\\\\..\\\\evil\") reaching BackslashUnpacker even when forward-slash checks are only applied elsewhere.\n  - NameMapper-rewritten names — the check must run on the final `name`/`file` produced after `mapper.map(...)`, not on the raw `zipEntry.getName()`, since a custom or default IdentityNameMapper can pass traversal segments through unchanged.\n  - Symlink-based escapes: canonical-path comparison (via getCanonicalPath, which resolves symlinks) is required rather than purely lexical normalization, otherwise a pre-existing symlinked subdirectory inside outputDir could still redirect writes outside it.\n  - Directory entries (zipEntry.isDirectory()) going through FileUtils.forceMkdir(file) must be checked too, not just file-copy branches — the alert traces include both the mkdir path (isDirectory) and the copy path.",
        "status": "pov_created",
        "summary": "Created a standalone Java PoV that builds a malicious zip whose entry name contains many \"../\" traversal segments, then calls the real public API org.zeroturnaround.zip.ZipUtil.unpack(File, File). On the current (unpatched) code, ZipUtil$Unpacker.process builds the destination file as `new File(outputDir, name)` with no containment check, so the entry is written to a fixed location well outside outputDir, proving CWE-22 (Zip Slip)."
      }
